          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The notion of
            %em rigged Hilbert spaces
            serves to treat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+theory"} spectral theory
            of normal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unbounded+operators"} unbounded operators
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            much as if it were about actual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvalues"} eigenvalues
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvectors"} eigenvectors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenstates"} eigenstates
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            as used in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
          %h2#example Example
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            be the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi L
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi d
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L^2(\mathbb{R}, d x)
            consisting of square integrable functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            with respect to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unbounded+operator"} unbounded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-adjoint+operator"} self-adjoint operator
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi m
                            %mi x
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mtd
                          %mi H
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⟶
                        %mtd
                          %mi H
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mo ↦
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo ⋅
                          %mi f
                          %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                            %mrow
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  m_x \,\colon\,
                  \&amp;
                  H &amp;\longrightarrow&amp; H
                  \\\
                  \&amp; f &amp;\mapsto&amp; x \cdot f
                  \\mathrlap{\,,}
                  }
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi y
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (x \cdot f)(y) \coloneqq y f(y)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This operator is not defined on all of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dense+subspace"} dense subspace
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schwartz+space"} Schwartz space
            consisting of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            all of whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^{(n)}(x)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rapidly+decreasing+function"} decay rapidly
            at infinity (more rapidly than any negative power of
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} |x|
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mo →
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: S \to H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_x
            is defined globally on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            Meanwhile the Schwartz space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            carries its own topology (as described in the article
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distribution"} distribution
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^*
            is defined to be the continuous dual of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+vector+space"} topological vector space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: S \to H
            is dense, it follows that any continuous functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: S \to \mathbb{C}
          %p
            has at most one extension to a continuous functional
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \to \mathbb{C}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi i
                    %mo *
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi H
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i^*: H^* \to S^*
          %p
            is injective. In addition, the topology on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            is such that the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_x: S \to S
            is continuous.
          %p
            In this example, there is a dense inclusion
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S \to S^*
            defined by the inner product pairing, and the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_x
            extends uniquely to an operator
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^* \to S^*
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_x
            by abuse of notation. Again, in this example, the operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_x: S^* \to S^*
            has an eigenvector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_{\xi}
            for each
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ξ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi \in \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi m
                    %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi ξ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi ξ
                  %msub
                    %mi s
                    %mi ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_x(s_{\xi}) = \xi s_{\xi}
          %p
            %em Ugh. Lousy start on something that would be nice to understand properly. Maybe an expert can help out. John, you there?
          %h2#references References
          %p Among the original treatises on the theory of rigged Hilbert spaces is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Roberts"}> John Roberts
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Rigged Hilbert spaces in quantum mechanics
              %em 3
              (1966) 98–119. doi:
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01645448"} 10.1007/BF01645448
          %p A unification of various inequivalent approaches is claimed to be achieved in
          %ul
            %li
              M. Gadella, F. Gómez,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Unified Mathematical Formalism for the Dirac Formulation of Quantum Mechanics
              %em 32
              (2002) 815–869, doi:
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1016069311589"} 10.1023/A:1016069311589
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              S. Wickramasekara, A. Bohm,
              %em Symmetry Representations in the Rigged Hilbert Space Formulation of Quantum Mechanics
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0302018"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            Rigged Hilbert spaces are also known as
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gelfand+triple"} Gelfand triple
          %ul
            %li Gel’fand, I. M., and Shilov, Georgiĭ Evgenʹevich. Generalized Functions. United States, American Mathematical Society, (2016)
          %h2#see_also See also
          %p Discussion on MathOverflow
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "https://mathoverflow.net/questions/43313/good-references-for-rigged-hilbert-spaces"}
                %em Good references for Rigged Hilbert spaces?
